All four harnesses researched against official docs. Here is the per-harness matrix.

# Harness isolation controls (official docs only, 2026-09-17)

## 1. Kimi Code CLI

**User skills dirs & override/disable** ([skills doc](https://www.kimi.com/code/docs/en/kimi-code-cli/customization/skills.html))
- Scans `$KIMI_CODE_HOME/skills/` (default `~/.kimi-code/skills/`) **and** `~/.agents/skills/` at user level; plus project `.kimi-code/skills/`, `.agents/skills/`, `extra_skill_dirs` from `config.toml`, and built-ins. Priority: Project > User > Extra > Built-in.
- **Per-session replacement exists**: `kimi --skills-dir <dir>` (repeatable) "Replace auto-discovered Skills directories … applies to this session only" — verified in [config overrides](https://www.kimi.com/code/docs/en/kimi-code-cli/configuration/overrides.html). `extra_skill_dirs` only *adds* dirs; it does not remove the defaults.
- **No global kill switch** for model auto-invocation of skills found in official docs. Only per-skill controls: frontmatter `disableModelInvocation: true` or `type: flow` (manual-only). `builtin_product_skills = false` (or env `KIMI_CODE_BUILTIN_PRODUCT_SKILLS=0`) only disables Kimi's *built-in* product skills.
- `~/.agents/skills` and `KIMI_CODE_HOME`: [data locations](https://www.kimi.com/code/docs/en/kimi-code-cli/configuration/data-locations.html) states explicitly: *"Generic `.agents` resources stay under the real OS home so they can be shared across tools… user-level generic Skills remain at `~/.agents/skills/`"* even when `KIMI_CODE_HOME` is set. **Flagged ambiguity**: docs say "real OS home" and separately that `HOME` is "used to resolve the default data path" ([env vars](https://www.kimi.com/code/docs/en/kimi-code-cli/configuration/env-vars.html)) — whether overriding `$HOME` in the spawn environment redirects `~/.agents/skills` is **not explicitly stated**; only that `KIMI_CODE_HOME` does *not* move it. No other env var for the `.agents/skills` path is documented.

**MCP** ([MCP doc](https://www.kimi.com/code/docs/en/kimi-code-cli/customization/mcp.html))
- User MCP config: `$KIMI_CODE_HOME/mcp.json` (default `~/.kimi-code/mcp.json`), merged with project `.kimi-code/mcp.json`; project wins per-name.
- **No flag/env to disable MCP or restrict it to an explicit config for one session** is documented. Available controls: per-server `"enabled": false` in `mcp.json`; per-server `enabledTools`/`disabledTools`; global `[tools] disabled = ["mcp__<server>__*"]` globs in `config.toml`; and `[[permission.rules]] decision = "deny"` per MCP tool name. `KIMI_CODE_HOME` fully relocates `mcp.json` (clean isolation if set to an empty dir). Note: docs warn a bare wildcard outside `mcp__` patterns never matches, so `disabled=["*"]` is not a valid "disable all MCP" idiom.

## 2. Claude Code

**MCP** ([MCP doc](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/mcp), [CLI reference](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/cli-reference))
- `--strict-mcp-config`: "Only use MCP servers from `--mcp-config`, ignoring all other MCP configurations." The MCP doc confirms: *"Start the session with `--strict-mcp-config`. Claude Code then uses only the MCP servers you pass with `--mcp-config`."* So **`--strict-mcp-config` with no `--mcp-config` ⇒ zero MCP servers** (all user-scope `~/.claude.json` and project `.mcp.json` servers ignored). Third-party bug reports ([multica-ai/multica#1111](https://github.com/multica-ai/multica/issues/1111)) corroborate that strict-without-config strips all MCPs.
- Per-server settings keys: `disabledMcpServers`, `enabledMcpServers` (user opt-out/in lists in `~/.claude.json`), `disabledMcpjsonServers`, `allowedMcpServers`, `deniedMcpServers`, `disableClaudeAiConnectors` / env `ENABLE_CLAUDEAI_MCP_SERVERS=false` ([settings reference](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/settings-reference)).
- MCP scopes: local + user servers live in `~/.claude.json`; project servers in `.mcp.json`. In `claude -p`/SDK runs, project `.mcp.json` servers load **without prompting** — so `--strict-mcp-config` (or `disabledMcpjsonServers`) is the documented guard for headless spawning.

**Skills** ([skills doc](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/skills))
- Personal skills dir: **`~/.claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`** — loads in all your projects. Also project `.claude/skills/`, plugin skills, managed/enterprise `.claude/skills/`, and claude.ai-synced skills (`~/.claude/skills/synced/`).
- **No flag/settings key to disable the personal `~/.claude/skills` dir for a session only** (verified absent from CLI reference and settings index). What exists:
  - `--bare`: skips auto-discovery of hooks, skills, commands, subagents, plugins, MCP, auto memory, CLAUDE.md (skills in `--add-dir` dirs still load). Sets `CLAUDE_CODE_SIMPLE`.
  - `--safe-mode`: disables CLAUDE.md, skills, plugins, hooks, MCP servers, custom commands/agents, output styles, etc. — but *managed* policy hooks/status-line/MCP/plugins/skills still apply. Auth, model, built-in tools, permissions work (unlike `--bare`, which is minimal-tool).
  - `--disable-slash-commands`: disables all skills and commands for the session.
  - Per-skill: `disable-model-invocation: true` frontmatter, or `skillOverrides` entry `"off"` / `"user-invocable-only"` in settings.
  - `disableBundledSkills` setting (bundled only, not user skills).
  - **Managed-only** `strictPluginOnlyCustomization` (array `["skills","agents","hooks","mcp"]`) blocks user/project skills, hooks, MCP from `~/.claude.json`/`.mcp.json` — managed-settings scope only, not available to a normal session flag.

**Relocating config**: `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR` env var changes where Claude Code stores its files (i.e. relocates `~/.claude`) — referenced in [settings reference](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/settings-reference) as a storage-location variable; `--settings <file|json>` overrides settings keys per session; `--setting-sources user,project,local` can exclude project settings. Note `~/.claude.json` (MCP user servers, toggles) lives in the config dir.

## 3. OpenAI Codex CLI

**Config**: user config at **`~/.codex/config.toml`** ([config basics](https://developers.openai.com/codex/config-basic/)); state root is **`CODEX_HOME` (default `~/.codex`)** ([config advanced](https://developers.openai.com/codex/config-advanced/)). Relocating `CODEX_HOME` therefore isolates config + auth + history.

**MCP** ([MCP doc](https://developers.openai.com/codex/mcp/))
- MCP servers are `[mcp_servers.<name>]` tables in `config.toml` (user or trusted-project `.codex/config.toml`); shared with ChatGPT desktop and IDE extension.
- Per-server: `enabled = false`, `enabled_tools`/`disabled_tools`. Per-invocation override: `-c mcp_servers.<name>.enabled=false` (dot-notation `-c`/`--config` documented in config-advanced).
- **No `--strict-mcp-config` equivalent and no single flag to ignore user config** is documented. The documented per-run escape hatches are `-c key=value` overrides, `--profile <name>` (layers `~/.codex/<profile>.config.toml` on top of user config — still loads user config), and pointing `CODEX_HOME` elsewhere. **Flagged**: I found no official "skip user config entirely" flag.

**Skills**: Codex does have a skills mechanism (referenced in [config advanced](https://developers.openai.com/codex/config-advanced/): "For shared defaults, rules, and skills checked into repos or system paths, see Team Config"; user skills under `CODEX_HOME`). **Unverified**: the exact user skills dir path and whether it can be disabled per-session — not confirmed in the pages I retrieved.

## 4. Gemini CLI

**Config**: user settings at **`~/.gemini/settings.json`** ([configuration](https://geminicli.com/docs/reference/configuration/)); layers: system defaults → user → project `.gemini/settings.json` → system → env vars → CLI flags. Only system file paths have override env vars (`GEMINI_CLI_SYSTEM_DEFAULTS_PATH`, `GEMINI_CLI_SYSTEM_SETTINGS_PATH`); **no documented env to relocate `~/.gemini` itself** — flagged.

**MCP** ([MCP doc](https://geminicli.com/docs/tools/mcp-server/))
- `mcpServers` object in `settings.json` (user or project scope via `gemini mcp add -s user|project`).
- Global restriction keys: **`mcp.allowed`** (allowlist of server names) and **`mcp.excluded`** (blocklist) in settings.json — this is a genuine restrict-to-explicit-set control.
- Per-server: `includeTools`/`excludeTools`, `trust`; `gemini mcp disable <name> [--session]` (the `--session` flag is per-session, non-persisted; state otherwise in `~/.gemini/mcp-server-enablement.json`); `/mcp disable` in-session.
